		(uuid "2a947fad-c993-48f0-8b5b-efc48ed3f863")
	)
	(text "Blue = small finger group (1–11)   ·   Orange = key notch (12–19, no fingers)   ·   Green = large finger group (20–75)\n39 GND fingers: every even finger 24–74 is GND, under the odd signal fingers.   Carrier: +5V on 20 + 22 only, ≥ 300 mA per module.\nPin 4 is the module's +3V3 OUTPUT (ST-Link VTref, ≤ 50 mA) — never drive it from the carrier.   USB 23/25: wired, not used.\nAll 14 GPIOs are 3.3 V logic (PA0–PA7 also analog in; PA4/PA5 3.6 V max).   Carrier rules: box on this sheet + MES4-CIF."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 234.315 170.815 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "3a02ba5b-c673-4695-a8db-42688af61d5b")
	)
	(text "DESIGN NOTES & CALCULATIONS — CAN & M.2"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 31.75 213.995 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2 2)
				(bold yes)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "50cabe9d-b75b-4da8-af1f-0dd2dac74f50")
	)
	(text "J1 M.2 FINGER MAP — what every edge-connector pin carries"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 234.315 38.735 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2 2)
				(bold yes)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "63671b74-7894-4e41-9e22-546e32caae6e")
	)
	(text "CARRIER MUST-FOLLOW RULES  (the module depends on them; full text in MES4-CIF §0)\n1   CUSTOM pinout: NOT a standard M.2 card. Use only on MES carriers.\n2   +5V only on pins 20/22: 4.75–5.5 V, ≥ 300 mA per module (worst case ≈ 240 mA).\n3   Pin 4 = the module's 3V3 OUTPUT: never drive it; carrier loads ≤ 50 mA.\n     Fingers 2/70/72/74 are GND here: never put 3.3 V on them.\n4   Input protection is the carrier's job: reverse polarity, fuse or e-fuse,\n     cable surge, +5V never below −0.3 V. The module only has the D2 TVS.\n5   No hot-plug: power off before inserting or removing a module.\n6   No back-powering: never drive a module pin (VCP, GPIO, I²C, NRST) while\n     pin 4 is unpowered.\n7   I²C1 pull-ups to pin 4 (+3V3) must always be fitted.\n8   ESD at every external connector. CAN: low-capacitance TVS ONLY, NO series\n     resistors (they weaken the dominant level). GPIO/SWD/NRST: TVS +\n     optional 22–100 Ω. USB fingers 23/25: not used, leave unconnected.\n9   CAN: CANH/CANL never share a connector or cable with a supply above 12 V;\n     all nodes on one solid ground (common mode −2…+7 V); exactly two 120 Ω\n     terminators at the cable ends; stubs ≤ 0.3 m at 500 kbit/s.\n10  All I/O ≤ 3.3 V (PA4/PA5 3.6 V abs). PA8 may read LOW at reset ·\n     PC15 must not source current · NRST may only be pulled LOW."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 132.08 163.83 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "72a8c917-ac59-4b05-823e-983f7b23b659")
	)
	(text "TOP SIDE (F.Cu) — odd fingers"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 234.188 44.958 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.5 1.5)
				(bold yes)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "72f85919-2806-4ad8-ad75-ae3ead2f687f")
	)
	(text "WHY 120 Ω AT EACH END OF THE BUS?  (R7 + JP1)\n• A CAN cable is a twisted pair with ≈ 120 Ω characteristic impedance.\n   A bit that reaches an open (unterminated) end bounces back as a\n   reflection and rings on top of the next bits → bit / CRC errors.\n• A 120 Ω resistor at BOTH physical ends absorbs the wave: no reflection.\n   Two in parallel → 120 ∥ 120 = 60 Ω measured between CANH and CANL.\n• Recessive (= 1): no node drives; the 60 Ω termination holds CANH and\n   CANL at the same voltage (Vdiff ≈ 0 V). The ≈ 2.3 V common-mode level\n   comes from the transceivers' own bias, not from the resistors.\n   Dominant (= 0): U3 drives CANH up / CANL down, Vdiff ≈ 2 V into 60 Ω.\n\nWHY A SOLDER JUMPER (JP1)?\n• Every module carries R7, but only the 2 nodes at the cable ends may\n   terminate. JP1 is OPEN by default → R7 is out of circuit.\n   Bridge JP1 (solder blob) only on the two END modules.\n• Wrong count:  1 terminator = 120 Ω → reflections, errors at 500 kbit/s\n                3 terminators = 40 Ω → drivers overloaded, small signal\n• R7 power: 2² / 120 ≈ 33 mW typ, 3² / 120 = 75 mW max in use (0603 = 100 mW OK)\n\nCHECK WITH A MULTIMETER (all power OFF, measure CANH ↔ CANL):\n   ≈ 60 Ω  → correct, both ends terminated\n   ≈ 120 Ω → only one end terminated (bridge the other JP1)\n   ≤ 40 Ω  → too many terminators (open the extra JP1s)\n   open    → no termination at all\n\nLAYOUT: CANH/CANL routed as a pair (0.10 / 0.25 mm, ≈ 118 Ω diff),\n   R7/JP1 and D1 close to the fingers; keep branch cables (stubs) short."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 30.734 149.606 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "7cc67e61-19d2-4f1c-8b75-862098216a45")
	)
	(text "M.2 2242 B-key card edge"
